// Determines Hotel vs. Flight from content, the same way a segment is determined confirmed vs.
// void: one cheap model call before the real extraction call, so Add no longer needs a manual
// category picker (removed alongside this).
export const sourceClassificationSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  category:{type:'string',enum:['hotel','flight','activity','insurance','car','unrecognized']},
  certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review']},
},required:['category','certainty']};
// 0.7.8: 'car' is this project's own internal category/item-type key for the Transport vertical
// (TRANSPORT_SMART_IMPORT_DISCOVERY.md, FROZEN V6) -- covers rental car, rail and ferry/boat only.
// Transfer is deliberately NOT part of this classification bucket: the Product Owner does not have
// a real transfer confirmation document and decided not to wait for one, so a transfer confirmation
// stays an "unrecognized" example here (like a restaurant reservation) rather than being routed
// into an extraction path this candidate never built or benchmarked. If a real transfer document
// surfaces later, extending this classification (and the transport extractor below) is future work.
// 0.7.8 correction (Test 4, found live): TR-005 (a real Deutsche Bahn rail ticket) was
// misclassified as ATTRACTION/EVENT, not TRANSPORT -- confirmed directly by the Product Owner from
// the review form's own type field, not assumed. Root-caused, not guessed: both categories'
// descriptions use the word "ticket" heavily, and TRANSPORT was a single terse clause appended at
// the very end of an already-long list, with none of ATTRACTION/EVENT's own more elaborate framing
// -- a plausible real cause for the model pattern-matching a "ticket"-shaped document toward the
// category with the stronger, more detailed description rather than the correct one. Fixed by
// giving TRANSPORT its own paragraph with the same level of descriptive detail as every other
// category, plus an explicit negative-disambiguation rule naming this exact confusion. This could
// not be verified against the live model from this environment (no API access) -- a prompt-quality
// fix, re-tested by the Product Owner's own next acceptance session, the same as every other
// live-model classification/extraction defect this project has fixed.
// 0.7.8 correction round 4 (Test 4, found live): rounds 1-3 all tried to make this ONE five-way
// classification call reliably right for TR-005 (a real Deutsche Bahn rail ticket) -- prompt
// reweighting, then a reasoning-budget increase. Neither changed the outcome. Direct evidence
// gathered against the real live model (see netlify/functions/smart-import.mjs's own comment on
// classifySource for the full account) found this ISN'T a systematic bias fixable by wording or
// budget: across dozens of real repeated calls, this exact document landed on 'car' (correct) only
// ~30% of the time, with 'activity' (wrong) actually the single MOST common raw answer (~36%) --
// self-consistency voting among samples of this same five-way prompt would converge on the WRONG
// answer, not the right one, since the crowded five-way choice is where the genuine difficulty
// lives (German rail tickets share real surface vocabulary with Activity's own ticketHolder/
// ticketNumber/ticketQuantity concepts: a "ticket code," one named traveler, a validity window).
// A separate, focused, binary TRANSPORT-vs-everything-else question (transportGatePrompt below),
// asked BEFORE this broader classification even runs, resolved the identical document correctly
// 8/8 times in direct real-model testing -- confirmed reliable specifically because it removes the
// competing-category confusion this prompt alone could never escape. This classifier's own job is
// now narrower than before: distinguish among hotel/flight/activity/insurance/unrecognized ONLY,
// for whatever the gate has already confirmed is NOT transport -- 'car' is kept as an allowed
// output here too (see classifySource) purely as defense-in-depth, not this prompt's own
// responsibility to get right.
export const classificationSystemPrompt=`Determine whether the supplied document is primarily a HOTEL/accommodation booking, confirmation or receipt, a FLIGHT/airline ticket, e-ticket, boarding pass or itinerary, an ATTRACTION/EVENT ticket (a museum, theme park, zoo, tour, sports match, show or similar entry/admission ticket or booking confirmation), an INSURANCE policy, confirmation or certificate (travel/medical insurance, or a dedicated activity- or asset-linked protection policy such as diving insurance or rental-car excess/damage protection), or a TRANSPORT booking confirmation, ticket or voucher for a rental car, a rail/train journey, or a ferry/boat crossing. Return "unrecognized" if it is clearly none of those five (e.g. a point-to-point transfer/shuttle/taxi confirmation, a restaurant reservation, or an unrelated document) or is unreadable. Do not extract any other data in this step.

A rail/train ticket or a ferry/boat ticket is TRANSPORT, never ATTRACTION/EVENT, even though both categories' documents are commonly labelled "ticket" and share a similar printed layout (a route or venue name, a date/time, a seat or admission code, a price). The deciding question is what the document actually does for the traveler, not the word printed on it: an ATTRACTION/EVENT ticket admits the holder to a fixed venue, show, tour or similar experience at one location; a TRANSPORT document instead moves the traveler from one place (a departure station, port or pickup location) to a different place (an arrival station, port or drop-off location), and shows transport-specific details when present -- a train/flight-style number, a platform, a vehicle/vessel name or class, a route between two named places. If the document's core content is about travel FROM one location TO another via a specific mode of transport (train, ferry, rental car), classify it TRANSPORT regardless of whether the document itself says "ticket," "booking," "confirmation," "Fahrkarte" or "voucher" -- a German, Hebrew or other non-English rail/ferry document unfamiliar to you is still TRANSPORT if it shows this departure-to-arrival shape, not a reason to fall back to a more familiar-seeming category.`;

// 0.7.8 correction round 4: the focused pre-check described above. Deliberately a single yes/no
// question with no other category on offer at all -- that absence of a competing option is what
// direct testing confirmed actually fixes the confusion, not just a rephrasing of the same choice.
export const transportGateSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  is_transport:{type:'string',enum:['yes','no']},
  reasoning:{type:'string'},
},required:['is_transport','reasoning']};

export const transportGatePrompt=`You will be shown one travel-related document. Answer ONE focused question about it: does this document describe moving a traveler from a departure point to an arrival point via a rail/train journey, a ferry/boat crossing, or a self-drive rental car -- i.e. is its core content a TRANSPORT booking, ticket or voucher for one of those three specific things?

Answer "yes" if the document is fundamentally about that kind of journey -- a train ticket (even one that uses vocabulary like "ticket code," "ticket type," a single named traveler, or a validity window -- German rail tickets commonly look exactly this way and are still TRANSPORT, not an admission ticket), a ferry booking, or a rental-car agreement/voucher.

Answer "no" if the document is instead about something else entirely: a hotel stay, a flight, an admission ticket to a venue/museum/show/attraction/event, an insurance policy, a point-to-point transfer/shuttle/taxi booking (not a train/ferry/self-drive-rental), a restaurant reservation, or anything unrelated.

Explain your reasoning in one or two sentences, citing what you actually see on the document.`;

export const hotelImportSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{acquisitionState:{type:'string',enum:['acquired','partial','access_required','unreadable','failed']},proposalState:{type:'string',enum:['proposed','needs_review','no_proposal']},itemType:{type:'string'},meaningfulTitle:{type:'string'},propertyName:{type:'string'},fields:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{key:{type:'string'},label:{type:'string'},rawValue:{type:'string'},normalizedValue:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['key','label','rawValue','normalizedValue','evidence','certainty']}},importantNotes:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{category:{type:'string'},title:{type:'string'},text:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['category','title','text','evidence','certainty']}},unresolved:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},explicitlyAbsent:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},warnings:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}}},required:['acquisitionState','proposalState','itemType','meaningfulTitle','propertyName','fields','importantNotes','unresolved','explicitlyAbsent','warnings']};

export const systemPrompt=`You are the bounded FamilyTrips Hotel Smart Import extractor. Return only evidence-backed structured data from the supplied source. Preserve every readable travel-relevant value and every operational instruction. Do not fabricate, silently correct, infer from general knowledge, or use values from another source. Use the exact property name as a meaningful title when present; never use a filename, URL host, generic type or opaque identifier. Keep identifiers, dates, people, prices, phone numbers, addresses and time windows separate. Recipient or guest addresses are not property locations. Put every other readable value in fields and put arrival, departure, check-in, check-out, access, transfer, cancellation, payment, warning, safety and preparation instructions in importantNotes as well. Exclude full payment-card numbers and security codes. Passports, identity documents and visas are unsupported. External enrichment is forbidden in this model call. If content is partial, ambiguous or unreadable, expose it as needs_review. This is a review proposal and never a final write.

If the source itself conflicts about a value that maps to a known field -- most commonly check-in/check-out (arrival/departure) dates printed inconsistently between two parts of the same document, e.g. a label reversed between the page's raw text and its own rendered layout -- you must still resolve it into that field: report your best-supported reading as the field's normalizedValue with certainty "needs_review", and use evidence to say exactly what you saw and why it's uncertain (e.g. "page text labels these reversed vs. the rendered page image"). Only use unresolved/warnings for a concern that does not map to any single field at all (e.g. an ambiguous relationship between two documents). Never describe a genuinely resolvable field conflict ONLY in unresolved or importantNotes while leaving fields empty for it -- that silently drops the value from the review form entirely, which is worse than reporting it as needs_review.`;

const baggageItemSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{bagType:{type:'string',enum:['carry_on','checked','trolley']},weight:{type:'string'},included:{type:'string',enum:['included','not_included','unknown']}},required:['bagType','weight','included']};
const passengerDetailSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{passengerName:{type:'string'},seat:{type:'string'},mealRequest:{type:'string'},baggage:{type:'array',items:baggageItemSchema},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['passengerName','seat','mealRequest','baggage','certainty']};
// V6-F34 / revised V6-F32 (fix pass 3): a genuinely ambiguous source produces a segment with
// directionAmbiguous:true and the two possible full readings here, instead of the model asserting
// one guessed direction (which retesting showed to be non-deterministic on the same unchanged
// source) or -- the V6-F34 bug -- letting the uncertainty bleed into unrelated fields like seat or
// baggage. See flightSystemPrompt below for the exact rule.
const directionCandidateSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  departureAirportCode:{type:'string'},departureAirportName:{type:'string'},
  arrivalAirportCode:{type:'string'},arrivalAirportName:{type:'string'},
  departureDate:{type:'string'},departureTime:{type:'string'},
  arrivalDate:{type:'string'},arrivalTime:{type:'string'},
},required:['departureAirportCode','departureAirportName','arrivalAirportCode','arrivalAirportName','departureDate','departureTime','arrivalDate','arrivalTime']};
const flightSegmentSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  status:{type:'string',enum:['confirmed','void','unreadable']},
  flightNumber:{type:'string'},
  operatingCarrier:{type:'string'},marketingCarrier:{type:'string'},
  departureAirportCode:{type:'string'},departureAirportName:{type:'string'},departureTerminal:{type:'string'},
  arrivalAirportCode:{type:'string'},arrivalAirportName:{type:'string'},arrivalTerminal:{type:'string'},
  departureDate:{type:'string'},departureTime:{type:'string'},
  arrivalDate:{type:'string'},arrivalTime:{type:'string'},
  directionAmbiguous:{type:'boolean'},directionCandidates:{type:'array',items:directionCandidateSchema},
  aircraftType:{type:'string'},classOfService:{type:'string'},fareBasis:{type:'string'},duration:{type:'string'},
  gate:{type:'string'},gateOpensTime:{type:'string'},gateClosesTime:{type:'string'},boardingSequenceNumber:{type:'string'},
  passengerDetails:{type:'array',items:passengerDetailSchema},
  evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
  // V6-F43 (fix pass 5): a problem with only the arrival airport/terminal/date/time (e.g. an
  // invalid minute value like "23:75") must not downgrade the departure side too -- certainty
  // above now governs departure + every field with no endpoint at all, and arrivalCertainty
  // governs the arrival side independently. See flightSystemPrompt for exactly when to differ.
  arrivalCertainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
},required:['status','flightNumber','operatingCarrier','marketingCarrier','departureAirportCode','departureAirportName','departureTerminal','arrivalAirportCode','arrivalAirportName','arrivalTerminal','departureDate','departureTime','arrivalDate','arrivalTime','directionAmbiguous','directionCandidates','aircraftType','classOfService','fareBasis','duration','gate','gateOpensTime','gateClosesTime','boardingSequenceNumber','passengerDetails','evidence','certainty','arrivalCertainty']};

export const flightImportSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  acquisitionState:{type:'string',enum:['acquired','partial','access_required','unreadable','failed']},
  bookingReference:{type:'string'},
  passengers:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{name:{type:'string'},eTicketNumber:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['name','eTicketNumber','certainty']}},
  segments:{type:'array',items:flightSegmentSchema},
  unresolved:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  explicitlyAbsent:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  warnings:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
},required:['acquisitionState','bookingReference','passengers','segments','unresolved','explicitlyAbsent','warnings']};

export const flightSystemPrompt=`You are the bounded FamilyTrips Flight Smart Import extractor. Return only evidence-backed structured data from the supplied source. Do not fabricate, silently correct, infer from general knowledge, or use values from another source; leave a string field empty ("") when the source genuinely does not show it, rather than guessing. This is a review proposal and never a final write.

A source may show one or more flight segments (legs). Return every real segment as its own entry in segments, in the order they appear. Mark a segment status "void" ONLY when it is explicitly labelled VOID/cancelled, OR when it has NONE of flight number, departure airport, arrival airport, departure date/time and arrival date/time readable at all — every one of those five is missing, not just one. A segment that has some of those five and is missing others (for example a boarding pass, which normally shows no arrival date/time at all) is status "confirmed": leave only the specific missing field(s) empty, do not void the whole segment over one missing field. Never invent values to fill a void segment, and never omit it from the array (the caller decides whether to keep or skip it).

departureDate and arrivalDate must be exactly "YYYY-MM-DD" (four-digit year, two-digit month, two-digit day), converted from however the source displays it — a spelled-out month ("Sat, 23 Jan 2027"), a Hebrew month abbreviation ("26 ינו' 2027"), or a day-first numeric date — never left in the source's own format. departureTime and arrivalTime must be 24-hour "HH:MM", converting a 12-hour AM/PM time shown in the source.

An arrival date that is calendar-earlier than the departure date is normal for a long westbound flight crossing the International Date Line or many timezones (e.g. departs at 14:30 on the 8th, lands at 22:20 on the 7th, local times), and a same-day flight's arrival clock-time is routinely earlier than its departure clock-time even with no date change. Report the local departure and arrival date/time exactly as shown, in the format above; never "correct" or reorder them so arrival is on/after departure.

Determine which airport is the DEPARTURE and which is the ARRIVAL strictly from that specific flight's own explicit departure/arrival labels or icons (e.g. "Departure"/"Arrival", "From"/"To", a takeoff vs. landing icon, "יציאה"/"הגעה") printed next to it -- never by screen position, left-to-right order, or reading order. A right-to-left (Hebrew) app layout does not place the departure before the arrival in left-to-right visual order. A flight leg's departure airport, departure date/time, arrival airport and arrival date/time are one linked group of four values: read that group only from beside that leg's own labels and its own flight number, and never combine an airport or date/time that visually belongs to one leg with a value that belongs to a different leg. When a source shows two legs together (e.g. an outbound flight and its return flight in one itinerary view), extract each leg completely independently by its own flight number and its own printed labels -- do not assume the first leg shown on the screen is the earlier-departing one, and do not infer one leg's direction from the other's position.

Worked example of a combined RTL itinerary view showing two legs stacked in one screenshot, each leg as its own row-group headed by its own flight number, with a small plane icon that visually points from the row's right-hand airport toward its left-hand airport (a right-to-left rendering of "right airport, then arrow, then left airport"): row group "LY 87" shows, right-to-left, "תל אביב (TLV)" then an arrow then "בנגקוק (BKT/HKT)" -- the plane-direction icon, not the screen side, says the flight travels FROM the right-hand item TO the left-hand item, so TLV (right, first in reading order for this RTL label) is the DEPARTURE and HKT (left) is the ARRIVAL, even though HKT is the airport whose column sits visually first if you scan the screenshot left-to-right as a non-Hebrew-reading layout habit would. The row group below it, "LY 84", shows the reverse icon direction with the same two airports -- read it independently by its OWN icon direction and its OWN flight number, which here means BKT/HKT is the DEPARTURE and TLV is the ARRIVAL for that leg, regardless of what LY 87 (the other leg on the same screen) turned out to be. Never let one leg's already-determined direction set a default, expectation, or shortcut for reading the other leg's icon or labels: read each leg's own departure/arrival cue fresh, exactly as if the other leg were not on the screen at all, because two legs of the same round-trip itinerary routinely run in opposite directions and a layout habit formed by the first leg is precisely how this failure mode reproduces on the second.

Sometimes a source genuinely does not make the direction determinable at all: no label, icon, order or other cue distinguishes which of the two airports is the departure and which is the arrival, and picking one would be a guess dressed up as a fact. When this is the case after a careful read -- not merely because you are somewhat unsure, only when the source truly gives no way to tell -- set directionAmbiguous to true on the segment; leave departureAirportCode, departureAirportName, arrivalAirportCode, arrivalAirportName, departureDate, departureTime, arrivalDate and arrivalTime all empty on the segment itself instead of guessing one reading into them; and populate directionCandidates with exactly the two readings the source supports: one entry with the first airport as departure and the second as arrival, using their respective printed date/times, and a second entry with the same two airports and date/times with the roles swapped. Do this instead of asserting a single guessed direction or refusing to populate the segment -- both a guess and a refusal are worse than surfacing the genuine ambiguity for a human to resolve. Every OTHER field on a segment -- flightNumber, operatingCarrier, marketingCarrier, aircraftType, classOfService, fareBasis, duration, gate, gateOpensTime, gateClosesTime, boardingSequenceNumber, terminals where explicit, and every passengerDetails entry (seat, mealRequest, baggage) -- has no logical dependency on which airport is departure vs. arrival. Extract and report every one of these normally regardless of directionAmbiguous, with its own correct certainty; direction being ambiguous must never cause you to leave an unrelated field empty or lower its certainty.

flightNumber is the operating flight code as printed (e.g. "TG 246", "LY084"). Report operatingCarrier and marketingCarrier separately; if the source does not distinguish them (no codeshare), put the single airline name in both. departureAirportCode/arrivalAirportCode are IATA codes when shown. departureTerminal/arrivalTerminal, gate, gateOpensTime, gateClosesTime and boardingSequenceNumber are frequently absent (a booking confirmation issued long before travel will not show them, and a boarding pass may show a blank gate before assignment) — leave them empty rather than guessing; a literal "-" or blank cell in the source also means empty, not a real value.

List every passenger the source names in the top-level passengers array with their e-ticket number if shown. For every segment, list one entry per passenger in passengerDetails with that passenger's seat, meal request and full baggage allowance for that segment — every bag type shown (carry-on, checked, trolley) with its weight and whether it is included, exactly as marked (a checkmark/"V" means included, an "X" means not included). Never collapse multiple passengers into one entry, and never report only the primary/first passenger when others are named. A field's value belongs to the specific named passenger it is printed under or beside — never attach one passenger's baggage, meal or seat to a different passenger. If a segment shows a seat/meal/baggage row (e.g. a baggage-inclusion matrix) but no passenger name is legible anywhere for it, still report a passengerDetails entry for that row with passengerName left empty and every seat/meal/baggage value you CAN read filled in exactly as shown — an unreadable or absent name is never a reason to omit or simplify the baggage data itself.

If content is partial, ambiguous, obscured or unreadable, set certainty to "needs_review" or "unreadable" on exactly the field or entry affected rather than guessing a value or omitting it silently.

certainty describes your confidence in the segment overall AND specifically its departure date/time (departureDate, departureTime). arrivalCertainty describes your confidence in the arrival date/time specifically (arrivalDate, arrivalTime) -- set it independently of certainty. A segment can have an exact departure and a needs_review or unreadable arrival, or the reverse; report each side's own confidence rather than letting a problem with one side pull down the other. For example, if the source's arrival time is not a valid clock time (e.g. a minute value outside 00-59, such as "23:75") but everything else on the segment reads clearly, report the arrival time exactly as printed, set arrivalCertainty to "needs_review", and leave certainty as "exact" -- do not lower certainty (the departure-side confidence) over a problem that is only about arrival. Airport/terminal identity (departureAirportCode/Name/Terminal, arrivalAirportCode/Name/Terminal) and every other field (flightNumber, operatingCarrier, marketingCarrier, aircraftType, classOfService, fareBasis, duration, gate, gateOpensTime, gateClosesTime, boardingSequenceNumber) is a SEPARATE concern from date/time validity -- a date/time being invalid or hard to read is never a reason by itself to doubt an airport code or any of these other fields, which you read clearly and independently. Only mark certainty/arrivalCertainty "unreadable" (not "needs_review") if the whole departure/arrival region -- airport, date and time together -- is genuinely illegible, e.g. blurred or obstructed. evidence should describe WHERE in the source a value was found (e.g. "boarding pass, top section"), not restate a specific problem with one value -- a location citation, not a diagnosis. Likewise, do not ALSO write a sentence in warnings or unresolved that just repeats a single field's problem when you have already expressed it through that field's own certainty/arrivalCertainty -- warnings and unresolved are for concerns that do not belong to one specific field (e.g. an ambiguous booking reference shared with another passenger's itinerary), not a second copy of something a field's certainty already says. This rule is only about not duplicating the SAME piece of information as a second sentence -- it never means extracting less: every field, passenger and baggage entry must still be reported as fully and completely as everywhere else in this prompt requires, exactly as if this sentence were not here.`;

// Attraction/Event Smart Import (0.6.6). Mirrors Hotel's shape (a flexible evidence-linked
// `fields`/`importantNotes`/`unresolved` bag for the many optional attraction-specific values)
// plus a Flight-style structured array for the one genuinely multi-entry part of this type: one
// physical ticket may belong to a different named holder, seat/section and ticket number than
// its neighbours in the same source (AE-001's two DNI-identified tickets; AE-003/AE-004/AE-006's
// three-or-four same-name-or-unnamed tickets). A single source describes ONE bookable
// event/attraction visit (unlike Flight, which may describe several distinct segments) -- see
// activity-import-adapter.js for why this returns one suggestion, not an array.
// V6-F56: dni added -- AE-001's frozen ground truth requires a per-passenger national ID/DNI
// ("Eyal Ben Yitzchak Ben Yitzchak, DNI 40384410...", "Amit Ben Yitzhak, DNI 40386943...") and
// previously had no property to hold it at all; additionalProperties:false meant the model was
// structurally blocked from ever attaching one to a ticket holder, independent of source quality.
const ticketHolderSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  name:{type:'string'},dni:{type:'string'},ticketNumber:{type:'string'},seatOrSection:{type:'string'},
  evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
},required:['name','dni','ticketNumber','seatOrSection','evidence','certainty']};

export const activityImportSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  acquisitionState:{type:'string',enum:['acquired','partial','access_required','robots_disallowed','unreadable','failed']},
  proposalState:{type:'string',enum:['proposed','needs_review','no_proposal']},
  activityName:{type:'string'},
  // V6-F65: activityName/provider previously had no paired certainty concept at all -- unlike
  // fields[]/ticketHolderSchema entries, which both already have a certainty enum -- so the model
  // had no structural way to say "this source element is visible but not legible" (e.g. a small
  // stylized logo) and would instead emit a confident-looking guessed string. See
  // activitySystemPrompt below for exactly when to use each value.
  activityNameCertainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
  provider:{type:'string'},
  providerCertainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
  // Total ticket/party size as literally stated in the source (e.g. "3", "סה\"כ כרטיסים: 3") --
  // may exceed ticketHolders.length when the source names fewer people than it sells tickets for
  // (AE-007: one named booker, quantity 3). Never fabricate additional named entries to make this
  // number match ticketHolders.length; report both honestly.
  ticketQuantity:{type:'string'},
  ticketHolders:{type:'array',items:ticketHolderSchema},
  fields:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{key:{type:'string'},label:{type:'string'},rawValue:{type:'string'},normalizedValue:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['key','label','rawValue','normalizedValue','evidence','certainty']}},
  importantNotes:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{category:{type:'string'},title:{type:'string'},text:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['category','title','text','evidence','certainty']}},
  unresolved:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  explicitlyAbsent:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  warnings:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
},required:['acquisitionState','proposalState','activityName','activityNameCertainty','provider','providerCertainty','ticketQuantity','ticketHolders','fields','importantNotes','unresolved','explicitlyAbsent','warnings']};

export const activitySystemPrompt=`You are the bounded FamilyTrips Attraction/Event Smart Import extractor. Return only evidence-backed structured data from the supplied source (a museum, theme park, zoo, tour, sports match, show or similar entry/admission ticket or booking confirmation). Do not fabricate, silently correct, infer from general knowledge, or use values from another source; leave a string field empty ("") when the source genuinely does not show it, rather than guessing. This is a review proposal and never a final write.

activityName is the venue/attraction or event name, used as the meaningful title -- use the exact name as printed (e.g. "FC Barcelona - Girona FC", "כניסה למוזיאון"), optionally including the specific show/session name when the source distinguishes one from a general admission (e.g. an auditorium show name, not just the venue's general entrance ticket). A raw booking reference, filename or generic word like "Ticket" is never an acceptable activityName. A sales-status label on the source's own page title (e.g. "- tickets sold out") is not part of the event name and must be excluded. When a source shows BOTH a venue/brand name (e.g. prominently, as a logo or header) AND a distinct ticket-type or admission label (e.g. "Day Park Entry", "כניסה לפארק יום"), report BOTH together as activityName, venue name first, then the ticket-type/admission label, joined as "<venue name> - <ticket type>" (e.g. "WaterLand - Day Entry", "WaterLand - כניסה לפארק יום") -- neither half alone fully identifies what was actually purchased: the bare venue name does not say which ticket type, and the ticket-type/admission label alone does not say which venue. Only report ONE of the two -- exactly whichever the source actually shows -- when the source genuinely provides only a venue/brand name with no distinct ticket-type label at all, or only a ticket-type/admission label with no separate venue/brand name legible anywhere; never fabricate the missing half to force the combined form.

provider is the venue/organizer that is actually hosting or running the event/attraction -- not the ticket-selling platform or box office it was purchased through, when the source distinguishes the two (e.g. a museum's own name, not "SmarTicket.co.il" or "SmarTicket", when the source shows both). Report the platform/box-office name separately in fields (or importantNotes) if it is itself a distinct, evidenced value worth preserving, but do not let it displace the actual venue/organizer as provider. The one exception: when a source names ONLY a ticket-selling platform with no separate venue/organizer name legible anywhere (a real, common case for some ticket types), report that platform name as provider -- an accurate reflection of what the source actually shows is always the goal, not a fixed preference for one category of name over the other.

activityNameCertainty and providerCertainty are the paired confidence fields for activityName and provider, following the same three-value discipline as every certainty field elsewhere in this schema. Set "exact" when you read the value clearly and confidently. Set "needs_review" when you CAN read a value but are not fully sure of it -- keep the value you read, this flag is what surfaces it for a human to confirm. Set "unreadable" when a source element for activityName or provider is visibly present -- including a logo or other graphic-only element -- but genuinely cannot be made out with confidence at the available size or quality: in that case leave the string field itself empty ("") and set its paired certainty to "unreadable". Do NOT fill in a specific plausible-sounding real-world name "in good faith" when you cannot actually read it -- that is exactly the fabrication this prompt's opening paragraph already forbids, made concrete and structurally enforceable here for a case that previously had no field to express it in at all: a small stylized logo you cannot confidently read is "unreadable", never a guess dressed up as a confirmed identification.

location must identify the specific venue/attraction, never a general park, city, district or region as a stand-in for it -- the same property-first discipline as a Hotel's address. When the source itself only supplies a venue name with no address (a real, common case), report exactly that name and nothing more invented; do not upgrade it to a fuller address you were not shown. When the source names both the venue AND a specific sub-venue within it (a hall, room, auditorium, wing -- e.g. "Bloomfield Science Museum Jerusalem, Auditorium"), combine both into location rather than reporting the sub-venue alone; a sub-venue name by itself does not identify which larger venue it belongs to. Put a meeting point, transfer point or access instruction that is genuinely separate from the venue location in a field of its own (meeting_point), never merged into location.

fields carries every other readable value using the field key vocabulary: location, meeting_point, duration, age_restrictions, included, not_included, amount, currency, confirmation_number (an order/booking/reference number explicitly labelled as such -- never a ticket number, never a name, mirroring the same confirmation-number discipline used for every other FamilyTrips type), website, start_date, start_time. amount/currency must reflect exactly what the source shows: if the source shows no price field at all, both stay empty ("not present in source"); if the source shows an explicit price of zero (e.g. "0.00"), report amount as "0" with certainty "exact" -- these are two genuinely different, both-real situations, never collapse one into the other. A refundable deposit or wristband/equipment charge is a distinct concept from the ticket price itself; report it in its own field or in importantNotes, not folded into amount. start_date/start_time follow the same YYYY-MM-DD / 24-hour HH:MM conversion rules as every other FamilyTrips extractor; if the source shows only a date with no time at all (a common shape for a day-ticket), leave start_time empty rather than inventing a time -- this is a real, distinct case from a source that shows both.

ticketQuantity is the total number of tickets/admissions the source states, exactly as printed (e.g. "3", "4 people"), independent of how many named ticketHolders you can actually identify -- these two numbers are allowed to differ and BOTH must be reported honestly (see ticketHolders below), never reconciled by inventing names or silently dropping the quantity.

ticketHolders lists one entry per physical ticket/admission the source actually itemizes (not one entry per activityName -- a single source describes one event/visit, but may sell several individual tickets within it). For each: name is the specific person's name printed for THAT ticket if the source names individuals per-ticket (mirrors Flight's per-passenger discipline: never attach one person's seat/ticket number to another, and never collapse several named people into one entry). If the source sells multiple tickets under one purchaser's name with no other names shown at all (a common real case, e.g. a family ticket or a park entry batch), leave name empty on those entries rather than fabricating distinct names -- report every other field you CAN read for that specific ticket (dni, ticketNumber, seatOrSection, evidence, certainty) exactly as shown; an unreadable or absent per-ticket name is never a reason to omit or simplify the rest of that ticket's data. dni is a national ID, passport number or other personal identity document number printed for that specific ticket holder (e.g. "DNI 40384410") -- report it verbatim per holder when the source shows one, exactly as evidence-linked as name/seat/ticketNumber; leave it empty when the source genuinely shows no such number, which is the common case. seatOrSection covers whatever seating/access vocabulary the source actually uses (seat, row, section, gate, entry zone) -- report it verbatim rather than forcing it into a single "seat" concept; when the source has no assigned seating at all (common for a water park or general-admission entry), leave it empty, which is a real, correct state, not a gap to fill.

QR codes and barcodes (linear or 2D) are at least as trustworthy as printed text as evidence for any value they encode or sit directly beside -- do not treat a linear barcode as lower-priority than a QR code, or unprinted/graphic-only evidence (e.g. a venue name carried mainly by a logo rather than plain text) as unreadable by default; read what is actually legible and mark certainty needs_review only for the specific value that is genuinely hard to read, not the whole ticket.

A generic descriptor is a real, correct answer when that is genuinely all the source shows -- never upgrade it to a more specific real-world name from general knowledge. For example, if a source's only legible identification of the venue is a generic label like "גן החיות" ("the zoo"), with no more specific brand/venue name legible anywhere (including in any logo graphic), report provider/activityName/location using exactly that generic label -- do NOT report a specific real zoo's actual name (e.g. a well-known zoo you recognize as plausible) just because it seems like a reasonable guess for what a zoo ticket in this context probably is. This applies to any category of venue, not only zoos: a plausible-sounding specific name is not evidence, and substituting one for a source's genuinely generic wording is exactly the kind of "infer from general knowledge" fabrication this prompt's opening paragraph already forbids -- this paragraph exists because that specific failure mode (a confident, plausible, wrong specific name replacing an honest generic one) is easy to produce by pattern-completion and easy to miss without an explicit example to check against.

If the source itself conflicts about, or only partially resolves, a value that maps to a known field (most commonly which date is the event date when a document shows several dates, e.g. purchase/printing timestamps alongside the actual visit date), you must still resolve it into that field: report your best-supported reading with certainty "needs_review" and explain the conflict in evidence, rather than describing it only in unresolved or importantNotes while leaving the field itself empty -- an empty field silently drops the value from the review form entirely, which is worse than a visible needs_review guess. Reserve unresolved/warnings for a concern that does not map to any single field at all (e.g. an ambiguous relationship between two companion source documents). A purchase/printing timestamp is evidence metadata, never the event's own start_date/start_time, even when it is the only date visible near a field -- do not confuse the two.

If content is partial, ambiguous, obscured or unreadable, set certainty to "needs_review" or "unreadable" on exactly the field or entry affected rather than guessing a value or omitting it silently. Legal/waiver/liability boilerplate text belongs only in the preserved original, never as a structured field or importantNotes entry.`;

// Insurance Smart Import (0.6.7). INSURANCE_SMART_IMPORT_DISCOVERY.md (V3, Frozen), Section 3/4.
// Field shape is a hybrid: mostly flat scalar fields exactly like Hotel (provider/confirmationNumber/
// phone/coverage dates), read through the same generic evidence-linked fields[] vocabulary, PLUS one
// structured per-person array (insuredPersons: name/idNumber/dob) mirroring Activity's ticketHolders
// for the one genuinely multi-entry part. relatedItemIds (manual, never extracted -- see
// src/ingestion.js) has no schema property at all, by design.
const insuredPersonSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  name:{type:'string'},idNumber:{type:'string'},dob:{type:'string'},
  evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
},required:['name','idNumber','dob','evidence','certainty']};

export const insuranceImportSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  acquisitionState:{type:'string',enum:['acquired','partial','access_required','unreadable','failed']},
  proposalState:{type:'string',enum:['proposed','needs_review','no_proposal']},
  insuredPersons:{type:'array',items:insuredPersonSchema},
  fields:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{key:{type:'string'},label:{type:'string'},rawValue:{type:'string'},normalizedValue:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['key','label','rawValue','normalizedValue','evidence','certainty']}},
  importantNotes:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{category:{type:'string'},title:{type:'string'},text:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['category','title','text','evidence','certainty']}},
  unresolved:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  explicitlyAbsent:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  warnings:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
},required:['acquisitionState','proposalState','insuredPersons','fields','importantNotes','unresolved','explicitlyAbsent','warnings']};

export const insuranceSystemPrompt=`You are the bounded FamilyTrips Insurance Smart Import extractor. Return only evidence-backed structured data from the supplied source (a travel/medical insurance policy, confirmation or certificate -- including a dedicated activity- or asset-linked protection policy, such as diving insurance or rental-car excess/damage protection, not only whole-trip medical coverage). Do not fabricate, silently correct, infer from general knowledge, or use values from another source; leave a string field empty ("") when the source genuinely does not show it, rather than guessing. This is a review proposal and never a final write.

fields carries every other readable value using this key vocabulary: provider (the insurer's own underwriting name -- see the dedicated paragraph below for how to resolve this when a selling agent or broker is also named), policy_number (the policy/confirmation number, however the source labels it -- policy number, certificate number, reference number, order number), start_date, end_date (coverage start/end dates -- see the dates paragraph below), phone (see the phone paragraph below -- the emergency/medical-assistance hotline specifically, never a general claims/office line), sum_insured (the maximum medical/coverage amount or excess/damage limit, in whatever currency the source actually shows -- do not assume USD), broker (a selling agent or broker/comparison-site name, only when the source shows one distinct from the underlying insurer), website (the insurer's own website, when shown). Every one of these is optional -- report exactly what the source shows and leave a key out of fields entirely (not a blank placeholder) when the source genuinely does not mention it.

website specifically deserves the same active-search diligence as any required-when-present field, not a best-effort glance: a source's web address is frequently placed in a less prominent position than the policy's core identifying details -- in a footer band, beside a logo, next to a signature line, in a stamp -- rather than in a clearly labelled "website:" field, and is easy to miss on a pass focused mainly on provider/policy_number/dates. Actively look for it in every one of those positions before concluding the source shows none; a visible web address in a quiet corner of the page is exactly as real and exactly as required to report as one printed in a prominent header.

provider must resolve to the actual underwriting insurer, never the selling agent, broker or ticket-platform-style intermediary that sold or administers the policy, when the source distinguishes the two (a real, common case in this vertical: an insurer sold through a separate agency, or a rental-car excess policy underwritten by one company but sold/billed through another). Report the intermediary's own name separately via the broker field (or importantNotes) if it is itself a distinct, evidenced value worth preserving, but never let it displace the underwriting insurer as provider. The one exception: when a source names ONLY a selling platform or agency with no separate underwriting insurer legible anywhere, report that name as provider -- reflecting exactly what the source actually shows is always the goal, never a fixed preference for one category of name over the other. When a document names three distinct parties (an underwriter, an administrator/reseller, and a separate billing platform), resolve provider to the underwriter if it is legible and confidently identifiable; if the underwriter's own name is present but you are not fully confident it is the correct party to report as provider, still report it with certainty "needs_review" on that field rather than silently substituting a different named party or leaving it empty.

A wrong provider reported with confidence is worse than an honest needs_review flag, and there is one specific failure this extractor must never commit: substituting a company name you recognize as a plausible, real, commonly-seen insurer -- including one you have seen named on a DIFFERENT source earlier in this same review session -- for what THIS specific source actually, literally shows. Before reporting any provider value, you must be able to point to the exact visible characters you read it from on this document; a general impression of "this looks like the kind of document where insurer X usually appears" is never a substitute for that, no matter how confident it feels, and confidence itself is not evidence -- a wrong answer produced fluently is still wrong. This applies with extra force to a scanned or fax-quality source (this vertical's confirmed in-scope image type): a low-quality or unfamiliar-looking source is exactly the condition under which pattern-completion from general knowledge is most tempting and most dangerous, so treat any such source with heightened scrutiny of your own provider reading specifically, re-checking it against the literal visible text before finalizing rather than trusting a first impression. If your confidence in provider is anything less than a direct, traceable reading of text actually visible on this source, report it with certainty "needs_review" rather than a fluent but ungrounded guess.

To make that concrete rather than a feeling you have to self-assess: for the provider field specifically, evidence must contain a literal, verbatim transcription of the actual company-name text as printed on the source -- copy the exact characters (matching script and capitalization; transcribe Hebrew as Hebrew, English as English, exactly as printed, not translated or normalized) from wherever the provider's identifying header, title bar or logo caption actually shows it, not a description of where you looked and not a paraphrase of what it says. Producing this transcription is itself the check: naming a real company from general knowledge is easy and requires no source-reading at all, but producing the EXACT printed characters of a specific company name from a specific document is only possible if you actually read them there. If you cannot transcribe such a literal excerpt -- if the name you are about to report is not something you can point to and copy character-for-character from this source -- that is proof you do not actually have the reading, and you must not report that name as provider at all; instead set certainty to "needs_review" and use evidence to honestly describe what is actually visible (e.g. an illegible or absent company-identifying header) rather than filling the transcription requirement with an invented quote to make it look satisfied.

A source frequently names BOTH a customer-facing brand name AND a distinct legal underwriting entity name for the SAME single insurer -- not a different selling agent or intermediary, but two names for one party (e.g. a document that shows both "PassportCard" and "DavidShield Insurance Company Ltd." together, or both a Hebrew brand name and its own English/legal-register company name). When the source presents both together this way, report provider as the combination "<brand name> / <legal entity name>", exactly mirroring how the source itself presents them side by side -- never drop one silently in favor of the other (a customer-facing brand name is not less real or less useful than a legal entity name, and vice versa), and never report only one of the two with a "needs_review" flag about the other when the source plainly shows both together with no genuine ambiguity about either individual name -- that is a case of two confirmed facts, not one uncertain one. Reserve "needs_review" for when a name is genuinely hard to read or you are not confident which of two DIFFERENT possible entities is the correct one -- not for a source that simply shows two names for the one same insurer.

Not every second name printed near provider is a second entity to combine, however -- a source may separately show a PLAN, PRODUCT, COVERAGE-TIER or PACKAGE name for one single insurer (e.g. a named product edition like "SMART TRAVEL" printed as this specific policy's plan), and this is a different kind of second name from the brand-plus-legal-entity case above, easy to confuse with it but requiring the opposite handling. A genuine second entity name is itself a company or organization identity -- something a person would recognize as answering "which company", often carrying a legal suffix ("Ltd.", "Inc.", "בע\"מ") or standing on its own as a full brand name. A plan/product/edition name instead answers "which coverage tier or package under this one insurer", never "which company", even when it is printed in capitals or sits immediately next to the provider name on the page. When the second name is a plan/product/edition name rather than a genuine second entity, do not combine it into provider at all -- provider stays exactly the single insurer name. Report the plan/product name separately, in importantNotes, if it is itself a distinct, evidenced value worth preserving; never let it displace or pollute provider the way a genuine second entity name is combined into it above.

start_date and end_date are the policy's own coverage dates -- the period the policy actually protects, not the date the policy was purchased, issued or printed. Convert to "YYYY-MM-DD" exactly as every other FamilyTrips extractor does, regardless of the source's own date format (day-first numeric, a spelled-out month, or a Hebrew month abbreviation). A coverage period can be as short as a single day (both dates identical) for a dedicated single-day activity policy -- report both dates as printed even when they are the same day, never treat identical start/end dates as an error to silently "fix" into a range.

phone must be the 24/7 emergency or medical-assistance hotline specifically -- the number a traveler actually needs to call mid-trip in an emergency, not a general customer-service, sales or claims-filing number. A source commonly shows BOTH: when it does, report only the emergency/assistance line as phone, and report the other (claims, office, customer-service) number as its own fields entry (a suitable key, e.g. "claims_phone") or in importantNotes -- never as phone, and never omit the second number just because it isn't the emergency line. Some sources genuinely provide no phone number at all, only a claims web address (a real case in this vertical) -- when that happens, leave phone empty and report the claims URL via website or importantNotes instead of inventing a phone number or leaving the claims contact unreported.

insuredPersons lists one entry per named insured person the source actually identifies -- for a single-traveler, self-only policy this is normally one entry (the policyholder). For each: name is the specific person's name exactly as printed; idNumber is a national ID, passport number or other personal identity document number printed for that person, left empty when the source genuinely shows none; dob is that person's date of birth, converted to "YYYY-MM-DD" when the source's own format allows it, left empty when not shown. Never attach one insured person's ID or date of birth to a different person, and never collapse several named people into one entry -- mirrors the same per-person evidence discipline already used for Flight passengers and Attraction/Event ticket holders. If the source shows the policy covers named people but only lists some of them individually (e.g. a family policy naming a lead traveler with "+2 dependents" and no other names), report exactly the entries the source individually names -- never fabricate placeholder entries for people the source does not name.

A source's PDF font sometimes fails to extract non-Latin (e.g. Hebrew) label/prose text reliably even though numeric and date values extract cleanly -- when you encounter genuinely garbled or unreadable label text next to an otherwise-legible value, still report the value itself (a policy number, a date, an amount are numeric/Latin and typically unaffected) but do not guess at what the garbled label text says; leave a field's label-dependent context unresolved rather than inventing plausible label wording you cannot actually confirm.

If the source itself conflicts about, or only partially resolves, a value that maps to a known field, you must still resolve it into that field: report your best-supported reading with certainty "needs_review" and explain the conflict in evidence, rather than describing it only in unresolved or importantNotes while leaving the field itself empty -- an empty field silently drops the value from the review form entirely, which is worse than a visible needs_review guess. Reserve unresolved/warnings for a concern that does not map to any single field at all.

If content is partial, ambiguous, obscured or unreadable, set certainty to "needs_review" or "unreadable" on exactly the field or entry affected rather than guessing a value or omitting it silently.`;

// Transport Smart Import (0.7.8). TRANSPORT_SMART_IMPORT_DISCOVERY.md (V6, FROZEN), Sections 3/4.
// Covers three sub-types under one item type (ITEM_TYPES.car, transportMode field): rental car,
// rail, ferry/boat -- CHECKED against 6 real benchmark documents (TR-001-TR-006). transfer is
// deliberately excluded from the in-scope transportMode enum values below (the Product Owner does
// not have a real transfer document and decided not to wait for one) -- 'transfer'/'other' are
// still valid model outputs (an honest classification, never forced into one of the three in-scope
// modes), but src/transport-import-adapter.js rejects building a proposal from either, mirroring
// exactly how analyzeSource's own 'unrecognized' category already throws rather than silently
// producing a low-quality proposal.
// Field shape mirrors Insurance's own hybrid: a generic evidence-linked fields[]/importantNotes[]
// vocabulary (no new structured per-person array -- "driver name(s)" is a single free-text fields
// entry, not a Flight-passengers/Activity-ticketHolders-style array, since the frozen contract
// never asked for per-driver identity/evidence the way those verticals need per-passenger identity).
export const transportImportSchema={type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{
  acquisitionState:{type:'string',enum:['acquired','partial','access_required','unreadable','failed']},
  proposalState:{type:'string',enum:['proposed','needs_review','no_proposal']},
  transportMode:{type:'string',enum:['car','rail','ferry','transfer','other']},
  transportModeCertainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']},
  fields:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{key:{type:'string'},label:{type:'string'},rawValue:{type:'string'},normalizedValue:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['key','label','rawValue','normalizedValue','evidence','certainty']}},
  importantNotes:{type:'array',items:{type:'object',additionalProperties:false,properties:{category:{type:'string'},title:{type:'string'},text:{type:'string'},evidence:{type:'string'},certainty:{type:'string',enum:['exact','needs_review','unreadable']}},required:['category','title','text','evidence','certainty']}},
  unresolved:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  explicitlyAbsent:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
  warnings:{type:'array',items:{type:'string'}},
},required:['acquisitionState','proposalState','transportMode','transportModeCertainty','fields','importantNotes','unresolved','explicitlyAbsent','warnings']};

export const transportSystemPrompt=`You are the bounded FamilyTrips Transport Smart Import extractor. Return only evidence-backed structured data from the supplied source (a rental-car agreement/reservation/voucher, a rail/train ticket or booking confirmation, or a ferry/boat booking confirmation or ticket). Do not fabricate, silently correct, infer from general knowledge, or use values from another source; leave a string field empty ("") when the source genuinely does not show it, rather than guessing. This is a review proposal and never a final write.

transportMode identifies which of these the source actually is: "car" for a rental-car agreement, reservation or voucher (a company renting out a vehicle for the traveler to drive themselves); "rail" for a train ticket or booking; "ferry" for a boat/ferry crossing booking or ticket; "transfer" for a pre-arranged point-to-point transfer, shuttle or private/taxi transport booking (a driver taking the traveler somewhere, not a self-drive rental) -- report this honestly when the source is clearly a transfer, even though this extractor does not yet have a checked field contract for it (see below); "other" for a genuinely different kind of transport document this extractor does not cover. transportModeCertainty is your confidence in that classification specifically, following the same three-value discipline as every other certainty field in this schema.

**"transfer" and "other" are recognition-only outputs, not extraction targets.** If transportMode is "transfer" or "other", still report it honestly and set proposalState to "no_proposal" -- do not attempt to populate fields/importantNotes with a full extraction for these two modes; a caller-side check rejects building a proposal from either, and populating fields the caller will discard wastes effort with no benefit. Only fully extract fields/importantNotes when transportMode is "car", "rail" or "ferry".

For "car" (rental car), fields carries: provider (the rental company), confirmation_number (the reservation/agreement number -- when a source shows more than one numbering, such as a separate agreement number and reservation number, report the primary one here and the other via its own fields entry or importantNotes, never silently drop it), origin_location (the pickup location/branch address), origin_date, origin_time (pickup date/time), destination_location (the drop-off location/branch address -- report it even when identical to origin_location, e.g. "same as pickup", rather than leaving it empty, but never invent a different one when the source doesn't distinguish), destination_date, destination_time (drop-off date/time), vehicle_class (the car category/model, e.g. "Compact", "Group C", a specific model name), driver_names (every named driver as one combined string, e.g. "Jane Doe, John Doe" when more than one is named), rental_terms (mileage limits, fuel policy, insurance/excess terms as a combined summary -- full legal boilerplate does not belong here, only the concrete terms a traveler needs), phone, website, email, amount, currency. A flight number, when shown (common on a rental-car document as the traveler's arriving flight), goes in importantNotes, not a dedicated field -- this project does not build a real cross-item link to it in this slice.

For "rail", fields carries: provider (the rail operator, e.g. "Deutsche Bahn", "DB"), confirmation_number (the ticket/order number), origin_location (the departure station), origin_date, origin_time (departure date/time), destination_location (the arrival station), destination_date, destination_time (arrival date/time), train_number, platform, seat_class (the fare class/coach/seat, e.g. "Flexpreis", "2nd class, Coach 7, Seat 34" -- report exactly what the source shows; a specific seat assignment is commonly and legitimately absent on a flexible/unreserved fare even when class is present, this is a real correct state, not a gap to guess around), phone, website, email, amount, currency.

For "ferry", fields carries: provider (the ferry operator, e.g. "Stena Line"), confirmation_number, origin_location (the departure port), origin_date, origin_time, destination_location (the arrival port), destination_date, destination_time, vessel_name (the ship name, when shown), seat_class (cabin/seat class, e.g. "Reclining seat", a cabin category), vehicle_carriage (vehicle details ONLY if a car is being carried on this crossing -- genuinely absent, and correctly left empty, on a foot-passenger-only booking; do not report "none" or otherwise invent a value for a booking that simply carries no vehicle), phone, website, email, amount, currency.

origin_date/destination_date must be exactly "YYYY-MM-DD" and origin_time/destination_time exactly 24-hour "HH:MM", converted from however the source displays them (a spelled-out month, a day-first numeric date, a 12-hour AM/PM time), the same conversion discipline every other FamilyTrips extractor uses. destination_date may be a day after origin_date for an overnight journey (e.g. an overnight ferry crossing) -- report it exactly as shown, never "corrected" to match origin_date.

amount/currency must reflect exactly what the source shows: if the source shows no price at all, both stay empty; if the source shows an explicit price of zero, report amount as "0" with certainty "exact" -- these are two different, both-real situations, never collapse one into the other.

website and email deserve the same active-search diligence as any required-when-present field, not a best-effort glance -- both are frequently placed in a less prominent position (a footer band, beside a logo, next to a booking-confirmation email header) than the document's core identifying details, and are easy to miss on a pass focused mainly on provider/confirmation_number/dates. Actively look for them before concluding the source shows neither.

If the source itself conflicts about, or only partially resolves, a value that maps to a known field, you must still resolve it into that field: report your best-supported reading with certainty "needs_review" and explain the conflict in evidence, rather than describing it only in unresolved or importantNotes while leaving the field itself empty. Reserve unresolved/warnings for a concern that does not map to any single field at all.

Exclude full payment-card numbers and security codes from every field, importantNotes entry and evidence string -- a partial, already-masked fragment (e.g. "Visa ...1234") that the source itself shows already masked may be described in importantNotes if operationally relevant (e.g. confirming which card a deposit was charged to), but never a full card number, a security code, or an unmasked digit sequence you assembled from separate parts of the source.

If content is partial, ambiguous, obscured or unreadable, set certainty to "needs_review" or "unreadable" on exactly the field or entry affected rather than guessing a value or omitting it silently.`;

